Hi @alisdair
You are totally right. I use for_each like this
```
resource "aws_security_group" "lambda_default_sg" {
  for_each = length(var.subnet_ids) > 0 ? {
    default = {}
  } : {}
  name                   = local.name_rfc1123
  description            = "${var.name} Default Security Group"
  vpc_id                 = var.vpc_id
  revoke_rules_on_delete = true
  tags = merge(
    {
      Name = "${var.name} Lambda"
    },
    local.common_tags,
    var.extra_tags
  )
}
```

Thanks!

I can reproduce the same error with the following minimal config:

```terraform
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

variable "subnet_ids" {
  type = list(string)
  default = []
}

variable "name" {
  type = string
  default = "repro-30127"
}

resource "aws_vpc" "default" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

resource "aws_security_group" "lambda_default_sg" {
  for_each = length(var.subnet_ids) > 0 ? {
    default = {}
  } : {}
  name                   = var.name
  description            = "${var.name} Default Security Group"
  vpc_id                 = aws_vpc.default.id
  revoke_rules_on_delete = true
}

output "sg_id" {
  value = lookup(aws_security_group.lambda_default_sg, "default", { id = null })["id"]
}
```

The underlying issue is that the `lookup` call is working with the resource value, which does not have the same type as the fallback value. I can't find the source of this change, but it is most likely in [the upstream `cty` library](https://github.com/zclconf/go-cty).

To construct a default value of the correct type would be quite unwieldy—you would need to specify `null` values for all the keys in the `aws_security_group` object type. Instead, you might want to use `try` instead:

```terraform
output "sg_id" {
  value = try(aws_security_group.lambda_default_sg["default"].id, null)
}
```

I'm marking this as confirmed for now, but it's possible that we might not be able to fix this, as it seems likely that this is the intended behaviour for `lookup`. Thanks again for reporting, and I hope the workaround above is helpful.
